{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, increase each NextItNet block's residual scale from 0.5 to 0.65. Since 0.35 regressed, modestly stronger convolutional updates may improve contextual modeling. Keep causal blocks, dilation schedule, features, embeddings, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Synchronize residual-scale metadata and predictor-writer checks; retain one checkpoint per category.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, change only loss reduction: average the existing positive-plus-negative softplus loss over valid positions within each sequence, then average over sequences. This gives short histories equal weight to long histories and may improve generalization given median histories of 7–8 items. Preserve sampling, topology, embeddings, scoring, readout, optimizer and training settings; retain one model per category.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, increase tied item embedding width and the corresponding hidden dimension from 48 to 64, propagating required shape changes without changing block structure or scoring. Greater representation capacity may capture more item relationships while retaining the parameter sharing that untied embeddings lost. Keep features, history policy, loss and optimizer fixed. Save the width in metadata and reconstruct matching predictor shapes; retain one model per category.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, strengthen history-item frequency attenuation from 1-0.3*feature to 1-0.4*feature, retaining feature=sqrt(log1p(training_count)/max_log_count). The regression at 0.2 motivates testing whether stronger suppression of common input items improves contextual discrimination. Use training counts only and preserve zero features for unseen items. Keep embedding design, topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed; synchronize predictor behavior and retain one model per category."
}
